# Lab network attachment definitions for the KubeVirt lab in namespace <%= namespace %>.
# Generated by SecGen -- fix lib/templates/kubevirt_nad.yaml.erb, not this file.
#
# One NetworkAttachmentDefinition per scenario network *interface*: SecGen gives every
# interface on a scenario <network> its own address (fixed IP_address, or auto-assigned from
# the network's range), and hands that address to other modules as <network_ip>. The lab is
# only correct if each guest actually ends up on the address the scenario was built with, so
# every interface gets a NAD pinned to its address. Interfaces on the same VLAN share one
# bridge, so the VLAN stays a single L2 segment.
#
# Shape, all verified against a booted lab VM on this cluster:
#   * `type: bridge` + `bridge: br-...` is the KubeVirt secondary-network CNI. The guest's NIC
#     ends up on that Linux bridge and takes the address the CNI gives the pod interface: a
#     guest behind this NAD reported `enp2s0 172.30.95.7/24` for a pinned allocation of .7,
#     prefix included.
#   * `whereabouts` IPAM with `range_start` == `range_end` pins the address. This is the only
#     way to do it: KubeVirt builds the Multus annotation for the virt-launcher pod itself and
#     DROPS an address a user asks for there (verified: an `ips` request on the VMI was
#     discarded and whereabouts allocated the first free address instead), and the VMI schema
#     has no address field for a multus network.
#   * whereabouts records its pools in a *cluster* object keyed by the range, so several NADs
#     may share one range with different range_start/range_end and each still gets exactly its
#     own address (verified: two NADs on the same /24 pinned to .5 and .6, both honoured, and
#     re-creating a consumer got the same address back). A pinned address is therefore not
#     "whereabouts may pick something else".
#   * The bridge is a *node-local* Linux bridge. Two pods or guests on different nodes but the
#     same NAD cannot reach each other (measured: 100% packet loss both ways), while two on one
#     node can (measured: 0% loss). A lab's L2 segment therefore only extends across VMs
#     co-located on one node, and the bridge name hashes the namespace so two labs on one node
#     never share a segment. See "Networking" in README-KubeVirt.md, and --kubevirt-placement
#     for keeping a lab on one node.
#   * A secondary interface gets NO default route and no gateway (verified): the guest's default
#     route stays on the masquerade interface, so a lab network cannot cut the guest off from
#     its node, and a scenario's default_gateway cannot be honoured by the CNI (there is no
#     router on the segment unless a lab VM is one).
#
# Two labs that use the same subnet at the same time share the same whereabouts pool and the
# same pinned addresses, and the second lab's interfaces will not come up. SecGen randomises a
# network's range per build, which makes that unlikely; a scenario with a fixed range needs a
# distinct range per concurrent lab (--network-ranges).
<% nad_networks.each do |net| -%>
---
# <%= net['comment'] %>
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata:
  name: <%= net['name'] %>
  namespace: <%= namespace %>
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: secgen
    secgen.io/role: lab-network
spec:
  config: '{
    "cniVersion": "1.0.0",
    "name": "<%= net['name'] %>",
    "type": "bridge",
    "bridge": "<%= net['bridge'] %>",
    "ipam": {
      "type": "whereabouts",
      "range": "<%= net['range'] %>",
      "range_start": "<%= net['range_start'] %>",
      "range_end": "<%= net['range_end'] %>"
    }
  }'
<% end -%>
